## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Since 1860s with the industrial revolution and the concomitant progress in urbanization over the decades involved burning of fossil fuels, which caused exponential increase in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ emission into the atmosphere. Over millions of years, plants assimilate $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into high energy compounds (carbohydrates) by photosynthesis for the benefit of mankind. However, accumulation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ back to the atmosphere at an alarming rate due to excessive consumption of fossil fuel has substantially influenced the natural carbon cycle [1]. It is correctly envisaged that the fossil fuel dependent energy demand will be extinct in the near future because of its increasing demand with economic growth worldwide. Biomass or organic waste cannot be a sustainable solution for clean energy system since these processes also produces a large amount of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . To reduce the atmospheric $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentration, sequestration and storage (CSS) via adsorptive removal of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ [2–4] as well as chemical fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into fuels and fine chemicals [5–7] has gained a widespread attention. For typical coal-fired power plant, adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ by amines costs around $59.1~\S/\mathrm{ton}$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ mainly because desorption and compression is an energy intensive process [8]. Thus $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion by utilizing the waste flue gas of the power plant at zero cost to convert it into fuel is an actively pursuing research domain and is preferable than CSS. $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion over conventional catalytic supports like carbon or metal oxides often suffers from serious drawbacks like coke deposition or considerable loss of active sites after several cycles due to poor metal-support interaction. In contrast, nanostructured porous frameworks are more flexible, contain large number of functional sites that prevent metal leaching and retain excellent catalytic activity after several cycles. Notably, the advantage of these porous materials in heterogeneous catalysis is largely observed due to the feasibility in designing materials bearing functionalized building blocks under different reaction conditions. COFs synthesized via Schiff-base condensation [9–11], base and acid catalyzed Aldol condensation [12] or ionothermal reactions [13], offer highly functionalized pore surface to carry out a range of catalytic/photocatalytic reactions. Porphyrin [14] and metalloporphyrin [15] units can be functionalized in POPs and MOFs with pre-designed porphyrin monomers [16,17] or in-situ condensation approach [18]. Due to the presence of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -philic porphyrin building blocks together with high surface area and robust framework structures, they offer huge opportunity as heterogeneous catalyst in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reactions. Similarly bipyridine [19], fluorine and triazine functionalized POP [20] and MOFs [21] are reported using Sonogashira coupling reaction [22], nitrile polymerization [23] or oxidative cyclization reactions [24]. All these functional frameworks are important since it may trigger ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and concentrate it to the nano-channels exploiting a higher substrate/catalyst ratio for the accomplishment of a successful hydrogenation reaction. Moreover, the functional support may facilitate strong interaction with the metal nanoparticles to tailor its electronic property, which is crucial for activating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule [25]. Importantly, catalytic sites can be easily grafted by forming stable covalent bonds in these porous materials together with the retention of catalytic property in their recycling process. Extensive research is thus devoted to designing stable and active catalyst with diverse functionality in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reductions.
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is a waste product of combustion and is safe, non-flammable, non-toxic as well as economical C1 source. Thus, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is highly demanding as precursor of high-value chemicals and fuels such as HCOOH, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , olefins and related petroleum products [26–28]. Noteworthy, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion to fuel could be accountable to $\sim30\%$ decrease of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , in contrast to only ${\sim}4\%$ via $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ fixation reactions [29]. Despite tremendous scope in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions over functional polymers and MOFs, MOF-derived carbons [30] or multicomponent photocatalytic systems [31] or electrocatalysis over supported nanocrystals [32] are often used in these $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ utilization processes. Furthermore, industrially it is also challenging to exploit $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ as a raw material for the synthesis of fine chemicals because of its considerable inertness and lack of well-known protocols for its activation. Practically there are too few catalysts that can be industrially suitable at minimum cost, high life time, simple and convenient preparation steps and high product selectivity while utilizing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ only as ambient source [33]. We found organic functionalization is indeed very helpful in tailoring a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -support interaction at the molecular level. Hence, in this review we have focused our discussions on advanced nanoporous polymers for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reactions (Scheme 1).
The synthesis of formic acid (FA) involves phase transition as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the gas phase reacts to form liquid. Thus the gas phase reaction is entropically unfavorable with positive free energy change $(\Delta G_{298\mathrm{K}}^{\mathrm{o}}=32.9\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}};$ . The reaction under aqueous condition is thermodynamically favorable with negative change in free energy $\langle\Delta G_{298\mathrm{K}}^{\mathrm{o}}$ $=-4\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ ) [33]. For the sake of the feasibility of the reaction, the thermodynamic equilibrium must be forwarded through a secondary process like pH, temperature, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ pressure and additives. It was reported that additives reacts with FA and may shift the overall equilibrium of the reaction. Thus if methanol or amines are added, FA synthesis would be favorable due to the formed methylformate or formamides in solutions [34]. Under basic condition, when the substrate is either $\mathsf{H C O}_{3}^{2-}/\mathsf C\mathsf O_{3}^{2-}$ , the reaction shows negative change in free energy and is favorable with $\Delta G^{\mathrm{o}}=-35\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . Although direct hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to formic acid is formidable, several reports demonstrate the hydrogenation of $\mathsf{H C O}_{3}^{2-}/\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ [35] using active catalysts and adding bases, amines, buffers for transforming formic acid to formate salts [36]. Ogo et al. have reported selective synthesis of formic acid (0.06 M) through direct reaction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [37] under acidic conditions by using water soluble Ru-complex.
Formic acid (FA) is an important chemical, preservative having antibacterial property and is widely applied in various industries as solvent, as reactant and even as precursor. Moreover, FA is a promising $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ carrier for energy applications and utilized in liquid fuel cell due to having less cross over problems and efficient oxidation kinetics [38,39]. Industrially, 800,000 T formic acid is produced every year from CO and methanol using very strong base under harsh conditions. But this process emits large amounts of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , which could be controlled if it considers $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions instead of CO-based protocol. FA synthesis via $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction is ideally atom economical [40], observed mainly with Ru, Ir, Rh containing expensive homogenous metal complexes [41]. In spite of excellent potential of the homogeneous catalysts for the CO2 to formic acid conversion, very limited cases are industrialized till now [42,43]. This is mainly because of the difficulty in getting pure FA from the product mixture, as well as huge energy required to purify it. Under homogeneous conditions, the dehydrogenation of FA is a common issue with precious metal catalysts. So, industrial synthesis of formic acid from $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ is highly desirable with stable heterogeneous catalysts to meet the global demand. Regardless of high activity, designing homogeneous catalyst requires critical challenge for making complex ligands [44] containing proton responsive groups [45]. Although nonporous supports [46–48] are often used low TOF, poor yield of formate, insufficient recycling are major issues, and these could be addressed by the catalysts based on nanoporous polymers. In the following sections we have highlighted major advancements made in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation process over COF, POP and MOFs.
## 2. $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ hydrogenation over functional polymers as catalyst
## 2.1. POP/COF
COFs are promising two and three-dimensional crystalline organic materials having periodically arranged pores of nanoscale dimensions and bearing chelating ligand sites for grafting metal complexes [49]. Heterogeneous catalysts comprising COFs as support may enable favorable mass transportation through utilization of perfectly aligned and periodically entangled active sites, connected via strong covalent bonds [50,51]. By virtue of abundant donor sites, COFs offer heterogenization of molecular complexes in the nanoporous frameworks that show high activity, durability and good selectivity for the desired products. Thus COFs, by virtue of having high surface area is considered as stable porous support and wider scope in the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reaction. Unlike COFs, in amorphous organic polymers, hierarchical pores are not periodically organized in the POPs, yet they display extremely high BET surface area and available functional sites that can efficiently bind with active metal sites, and these are very effective to activate $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules. Microporous polymers containing Trögers base (TB) as a built-in motif was prepared by Yang et al. [52] and later was subjected to modify with $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{I I I})$ complex, forming Ru-TB-MOP. Trögers base has unique structural features having rigidity, $C_{2}$ symmetry that was used to design
MOP with controlled specificity and orientation for exhibiting high selectivity during catalytic applications [53]. Schematically the synthesis of TB-MOP is shown in Fig. 1. TB-MOP possesses high BET surface area, $802\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as well as $0.5\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of pore volume, which is advantageous in developing Ru-TB-MOP. The presence of tertiary nitrogen at the TBsite enables strong interaction with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ as evidenced from high isosteric heat of adsorption $(\mathrm{Q}_{s\mathrm{t}})$ of $27.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . This feature is significant for concentrating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at the active sites of the microporous polymer, enabling more scope for the reaction. Hence, this metalloligand has significant TON of 2254 at $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in the presence of toxic $\mathrm{PPh}_{3}$ . Without phosphine additives, Ru-TB-MOP is apparently inactive (TON is 25) for hydrogenating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . This research thus pointed the dual role of TBfunctionality, forming stable complex with Ru, as well as accumulating enough $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to ensure high substrate concentration at the reaction centers. Interestingly, the grafted ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ in the microporous framework shows higher activity than homogeneous ${\mathrm{RuCl}}_{3}$ that clearly signifies the synergetic effect among TB-functionality and the micropores to promote $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation.
COF type 2D-layered ordered heptazine-based framework (HBF-2) bearing heptazine building units [54] upon immobilization of Ir(III) resulted very efficient catalyst for the catalytic hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into FA in a framework exhibited better TON of 6400 with total pressure of 8 Mpa $(\mathsf{C O}_{2};\mathrm{H}_{2}=1{:}1$ ) at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ reaction [55]. Here aqueous trimethylamine solution was used as reaction medium for easy isolation of FA- $.\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ adduct. They compared the activity with similarly designed $"\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}"\$ containing heptazine units (Fig. 2). Moreover, HBF-2 containing Ir/heptazine is more active catalyst with higher recyclability than $\mathrm{Ir/}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . The difference could be attributed to the mesoporous $\mathrm{19.6\nm}\mathrm{)}$ nature of HBF-2 in contrast to microporous (average pore size $=0.59$ nm) nature of $\mathrm{Ir/g–C_{3}N_{4}}$ . In general, higher mesoporosity of HBF-2 facilitates better diffusion of substrates to the active sites, which is somehow retarded in microporous $\mathrm{Ir}/\mathrm{g}–\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .
On the other hand, HBF-2 displayed much higher activity than homogeneous ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ and this could be attributed to the stabilization of active Ir(III) sites offered by heptazine moieties within the organic framework. XPS investigation also suggests, the improved electron density on Ir-site might be crucial to promote the catalytic activity in HBF-2.
Certainly, phenanthroline is a strong donor ligand under homogeneous condition, which may suffer prompt deactivation owing to its more open coordination sites [57]. Therefore, the POP synthesized by using monomer bearing phenanthroline moieties undergoes Sonogashira C-C cross-coupling in the presence of Pd-catalyst or Friedel-Crafts polycondensation in the presence of ${\mathsf{A l C l}}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}$ to yield rigid alkyne to flexible methylene containing POPs, as shown in Fig. 3. More importantly, after immobilization of $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{III})$ resulted IrPhen as a stable and chemically robust POP framework. It displayed excellent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion to formate in $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ with TON of 14,400 ( $120^{\circ}\mathsf C_{\mathsf{r}}$ 8 Mpa total pressure, $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ ), compared to other Ir-catalysts [58,59]. Moreover, it displayed very good recycling efficiency in this ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction process.
Recently Shao, Mioa and others [60] prepared Pd loaded pyridine and amide-based porous organic polymer, shown in Fig. 4. Owing to the electron donating sites at the surface, Pd NPs are electron rich on these POPs, and shows strong interaction with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ during hydrogenation reactions. The resulting Pd/AP-POP shows TON of 1279 at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ with $\mathsf{p H}_{2}/\mathsf{p C O}_{2}$ (4/4) which is higher than $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ (694) and Pd/AC (302) based on surface exposed Pd atoms. At this condition, the formate yield was found to be $83\%$ ( $\cdot\tt E t_{3}N)$ and is higher than the homogeneous princer complex $(70\%)$ [61]. Notably, catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}/$
AP-POP (TON 128) is close to other homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts in water. As for example higher than $0.6\ \mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ (TON 24, $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ with $\mathsf{p H}_{2}/\mathsf{p C O}_{2}$ (25/25) [62]. In fact, electron donating nature of pyridine in the POP is crucial for improving the activity of Pd and thus showing an impact in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction to formate [63]. Yamashita and others reported ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion to formic acid with bimetallic nanoparticles incorporated in benzylamine-based POP (amine-RF), shown in Fig. 5 [64]. Through X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) and X-ray photoelectron (XPS) spectroscopy, they showed that Pd-N-type structure is formed at $1.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ instead of any Pd-Pd bonding. Thus the oxidized nitrogen containing phenylmethanimine, phenylimine and phenylamine at the POP surface is restricting any growth of Pd nanoparticle and simultaneously prevents the oxidation of $\mathsf{A g}$ at the surface. Hence, the amine-RF is unique in developing bimetallic alloy through preventing self-oxidation and agglomeration at the surface. $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation was conducted at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with $\mid\mathrm{MNaHCO_{3}}$ at $\mathrm{pH}8.5$ in $2.0\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mpa}}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}:\mathsf{C O}_{2}=1:1$ , volume ratio) pressure which revels the strong synergetic influence between $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}A g}$ alloy and amine-sites, showing TON of 867 with small nanoparticles (size ${<}1~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ). The DFT analysis suggests higher adsorption energy between phenylamine moiety and ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ which is formed due to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ dissolution in $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Et}_{3}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}.}\end{array}$ This indicates that presence of the phenylaniline in the POP network has a positive effect for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formate.
On the other hand, aminal-rich POPs are also reported as an excellent support material for loading ${\mathrm{RuCl}}_{3}$ to execute $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions. The desired catalyst was prepared through Schiff-base condensation between terephthaldehyde and melamine at $180~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in DMSO and subsequently loaded with Ru (Fig. 6) [65]. The framework aminal (NH-C-NH) having electron donating property is significant to show ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ activation reactions with TON of 107 (2 h) is observed for base free hydrogenation. However, TON increases to 4492 after adding additive which could facilitates the heterolytic hydrogen-splitting during the catalysis [66], by forming zwitterions [67]. Interestingly, RuMPN shows the very high TON (2932) at an optimum Ru concentration of $0.83\mathrm{wt\%}$ However, TON decreases by increasing Ru content (1.08 wt $\%$ ). This result is clearly signifying that all functional sites in the microporous MPN are not suitable for coordination, Although the framework showed a high concentration of nitrogen (40.9 atomic%). High micropore volume may restrict metal loading and only chelating nitrogen that remains at the surface involves coordination with ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ . Under drastic reaction conditions, i.e., at $100{-}120^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ and 80 bar total pressure, Ru-MPN showed sufficient stability. Ru-MPN catalyzed $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation via formation of Ru-H through heterolytic cleavage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in a rate limiting step, as supported from the DFT calculation [68].
## 2.2. Covalent Triazine Framework (CTF)
However, the reported catalytic performance of CTF was studied under batch reaction conditions where $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation may prevail from the leached catalyst under homogeneous conditions. Thus, in spite of the robust nature of POPs/CTFs and the sufficient stability of the catalyst formed, batch reactions are not fully trustworthy. Under such circumstances, it is more desirable to consider the reaction under a continuous operational condition, which would be closer to industrial condition. Recently Urakawa and others [74] showed the continuous synthesis of formic acid and methyl formate using CTF catalysts under 300 bar pressure to evaluate its stability under such drastic conditions.
## 2.3. $C O_{2}$ hydrogenation to DMF over supported POPs
In a recent work, Yoon and others [77] showed a batch preparation protocol for N,N-dimethyl formamide (DMF) from $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ using phosphine-based POP. The authors prepared an efficient Ru-grafted bisphosphine POP (Ru@PP-POP), shown in Fig. 9 that displayed very high TON of 160 000, with high initial TOF, $29000\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Considering the significance of DMF as a common organic solvent as well as an important reagent for the synthesis of a wide range of fine chemicals [78], it is inevitable to find an alternative protocol for bulk synthesis of DMF. Industrially DMF is produced through the reaction between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and dimethylamine under high temperature $(150-350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and very high pressure $(30{-}100~\mathrm{MPa})$ [79]. Over Ru@PP-POP, the synthesis of DMF can proceeds very smoothly at $140^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a total pressure of the reaction mixture at $8.0\mathrm{Mpa}$ consisting of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and dimethylamine taken in methanol. The reaction proceeds through the formation of Ru-H bond at the catalyst surface followed by insertion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , which generates Ru-formate species and this undergoes oxidative addition by $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and regenerate Ru-H while eliminating formate (Fig. 9). In the final step, dehydration of formate produces DMF. The authors used specially designed trickle-bed reactor (TBR) for continuous synthesis of DMF at $140^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ , $12\mathrm{MP_{a}}$ pressure containing $0.5{\mathrm{~g~}}$ catalyst which executes high durability (60 h) as well as excellent productivity (915 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ gactive $\mathrm{\metal}^{-1}$ ) towards DMF, exceeding industrial methanol production (5.45 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ g-a1ctive metal) [80]. Nonetheless, $\mathtt{R u@}$ porous polymer is highly competitive than $\mathsf{C u A l O_{x}}$ [81], $\mathrm{[RuCl_{2}\{P m e_{2}(C H_{2})_{2}S i(O e t)_{3}\}_{3}]}$ [82], and Ir/graphene oxide [83].
## 2.4. Mesoporous Carbon Nitride
Lee et al. showed that mesoporous graphitic carbon nitride (mpg$\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ ) as a suitable support for Pd nanoparticles (Pd) and highly selective hydrogenation catalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to formic acid formation (Fig. 10) [84]. The authors suggested that nitrogen rich mpg- ${\cdot}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ may provide the adsorption sites for Pd ion without any additional ligands for its stabilization and hence forms uniformly dispersed ${\sim}1.7\ \mathrm{nm\Pd}$ on mpg- $\mathrm{.}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ [85]. The authors could obtain maximum $4.74\ \mathrm{\mmol}$ formic acid (FA) from the optimum condition using 13 bar $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , 27 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ They noticed that FA yield was not so altered by decreasing $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ pressure, while FA yield significantly deviates by lowering $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure. This investigation was further supported by $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ NMR, and they found the formation of $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathsf{N}.\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adduct could be responsible for the high solubility of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . The authors noticed, the pressure of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the reaction system is crucial to get high yield of FA. It was found that $\mathrm{Pd/mpg–C_{3}N_{4}}$ is two times more active than $\mathsf{P d/C}$ under the same conditions, demonstrating the role of graphitic nitrogen for activating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , which is in accordance with prior results about $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ activation reaction with benzene or olefin [86].
